.Dd October 21, 2026
.Dt FSIMG 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fsimg
.Nd create, inspect, verify and safely extract filesystem images
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm c Ns Op Cm W
.Fl f Ar image
.Fl F Ar stack
.Op options
.Op Fl C Ar dir
.Ar operand ...
.Nm
.Cm x
.Fl f Ar image
.Op options
.Op Fl C Ar dir
.Op Ar member ...
.Nm
.Cm x
.Fl O
.Fl f Ar image
.Op options
.Ar member
.Nm
.Cm t
.Fl f Ar image
.Op options
.Op Ar member ...
.Nm
.Cm W
.Fl f Ar image
.Op options
.Nm
.Cm i
.Fl f Ar image
.Op options
.Nm
.Fl Fl formats
.Op Fl Fl json
.Op Fl v
.Nm
.Fl h | V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads and creates FAT12, FAT16, FAT32, ordinary one-FAT exFAT, classic
QNX6 Power-Safe and QNX4 volumes and QNX ImageFS images.
FAT volumes with more than two FAT copies are reported as unsupported.
It requires no root privileges, mounts, kernel filesystem driver or external
filesystem utility.
It never modifies an existing image in place.
.Pp
The command is shaped like
.Xr tar 1 :
one mode letter chooses the operation,
.Fl f
names the image and the remaining words are operands.
.Bl -tag -width "-F stack"
.It Cm c
Create an image from host operands.
.It Cm x
Extract members into a fresh directory, or one member to stdout with
.Fl O .
.It Cm t
List members, recursively by default.
.It Cm W
Verify an image; as
.Cm cW ,
verify the staged image before it is published.
.It Cm i , Fl Fl info
Report the stack, the partition table and the volume information.
.It Fl Fl formats
Report compiled and usable capabilities; takes no image.
.El
.Pp
Exactly one mode is given, except that
.Cm c
and
.Cm W
combine.
Each mode may also be written as an option, such as
.Fl x .
The first argument, and only the first, may bundle mode and option letters
without a leading dash, as in
.Ql fsimg xvf boot.img .
Every character of such a bundle must be an option letter and one of them a
mode or
.Fl h .
The bundle is read as one option per letter, in order, and a letter that
takes an argument consumes the next unused word after the bundle:
.Ql fsimg cfF boot.img fat32
is
.Ql fsimg -c -f boot.img -F fat32 .
.Pp
Image paths, called members, are UTF-8 paths below the image root, relative
.Pq Pa EFI/BOOT
or absolute
.Pq Pa /EFI/BOOT ,
both naming the same entry.
Selection follows filesystem case rules and FAT short aliases.
Displayed paths retain their image spelling.
Several members select the union of their subtrees, and an entry selected
twice, by nested, repeated or aliased members, is processed once.
No member selects the whole image; a member matching nothing fails.
.Pp
All image parsing occurs in a fresh worker.
The coordinator prepares capabilities, supervises output and publishes
completed images without replacement.
The default sandbox policy uses available OS confinement and warns if
unavailable.
Unexpected confinement failures never trigger an unrestricted retry.
The library API does not sandbox its embedding application.
.Sh COMMON OPTIONS
Options precede operands on every host: option parsing stops at the first
operand, so a later word beginning with a dash is an operand.
The one exception is
.Fl C Ar dir
among the operands of
.Cm c .
.Fl Fl
ends option parsing; every later word is an operand.
No mode, two modes, an option the mode does not accept, a repeated singleton
option, a repeated setting or limit key (even with an equal value), an unknown
stack or setting name and an unexpected operand are syntax errors, reported
before any image or source is opened.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f Ar image
The image: input for
.Cm x , t , W
and
.Cm i ,
a regular file or finite read-only disk device opened without following a
final symbolic link, or
standard input for
.Ar - ;
output for
.Cm c ,
a pathname that must not exist, or standard output for
.Ar - .
Required except with
.Cm c Fl n .
.Pp
Disk inputs include macOS block and raw disk nodes, Linux block devices,
and FreeBSD disk providers. They are read directly without a payload copy;
compressed contents retain their spool requirements.
The invoking user needs permission to read the device.
Named FIFOs, terminals, sockets and non-disk special files are refused.
Standard input retains stream semantics even if redirected from a disk.
.Pp
Keep the input medium stable and writable output and scratch storage on
separate backing storage. Device-node metadata cannot prove that a mounted
filesystem or another writer has left the bytes unchanged.
fsimg does not create snapshots, enumerate mounts or change device state.
A whole-disk descriptor grants the worker access to the entire disk;
partition selection narrows parser operations, not descriptor authority.
The MBR profile requires a device with 512-byte logical sectors; GPT sector
size must match the raw device. Byte-range selection bypasses the table.
Creation uses fresh regular files or standard output.
Device names without an image suffix require
.Cm x Fl E Fl C Ar empty-directory
for directory extraction, under the ordinary destination checks.
.It Fl F Ar stack
The image's layers, inner to outer, separated by
.Ql / ;
the filesystem name is fat12, fat16, fat32, exfat, qnx6, qnx4 or ifs.
Required for
.Cm c .
The slots are the filesystem, the partition scheme (gpt or mbr) and the
container (dmg, vhd, simg or ifsboot, or the read-only hbcifs), in that
order, so
.Ql fat32/gpt
is a FAT32 volume in a GPT partition.
An ifsboot or hbcifs container carries one ImageFS and no partition
table: a stack that names a scheme or another filesystem with it, or
forces one around a detected one, is invalid.
On reading a named slot forces that layer while retaining every validation;
an unnamed slot is detected, and detection must be unambiguous.
.Ql -
marks a slot empty, so
.Ql fat32/-/-
names a bare volume.
.It Fl h
With a mode, display that mode's synopsis, options and examples on stdout;
alone, display the overview.
Without arguments, the overview goes to stderr and the command exits 2.
.It Fl Fl json
Write streaming newline-delimited JSON, schema version 1.
.Cm x Fl O
rejects it.
.It Fl l Ar key=value
Set a resource limit; repeatable, each key at most once.
Keys are scratch, spool, entries, depth, path, fds, output, work and seconds.
Scratch, spool, path and output use bytes; the others are unsigned decimal
counts.
Zero selects the documented default.
Seconds sets an operation deadline; zero adds no deadline.
.It Fl m Ar bytes
Aggregate library memory budget; default 256 MiB, minimum 16 MiB.
.It Fl o Ar key=value
A typed filesystem setting; repeatable, each key at most once.
On reading, only
.Ar oem=cp437 ,
the FAT short-name codepage, and
.Ar gpt:sector=512|4096 ,
which forces the GPT sector size and requires gpt in
.Fl F ,
are accepted.
Creation settings are described under
.Sx CREATION .
A key qualified by a layer name, such as
.Ar gpt:type=esp ,
names a layer the stack must contain; the partition-scheme keys are
described under
.Sx CREATION .
.It Fl S Ar auto|required|off
Select sandbox policy.
Required mode refuses an unavailable backend; off explicitly disables
confinement.
.It Fl T Ar directory|-
Scratch parent, defaulting to TMPDIR when set, otherwise
.Pa /tmp .
A dash disables scratch, and with it the spool, so neither a compressed
image nor an hbcifs container can be read; with
.Fl f Ar - ,
or with
.Cm cW
of a compressed image, it is a syntax error.
Fresh private scratch contains metadata, the unlinked spool described under
.Sx READING
and, for an image written to standard output, the unlinked raw file described
under
.Sx CREATION ,
never a copy of a source tree.
Use disk-backed storage for massive catalogs and spooled images.
.It Fl v
Verbosity, repeatable as
.Fl vv .
In modes
.Cm c
and
.Cm x ,
.Fl vv
reports bounded progress, capability details and sampled metadata-loss
examples on stderr; a single
.Fl v
alone adds no output in these modes.
In mode
.Cm t ,
.Fl v
selects the long listing on stdout and
.Fl vv
adds the same stderr details.
In modes
.Cm W , i
and
.Fl Fl formats ,
.Fl v
shows capability, geometry and sandbox details on stderr.
.It Fl V
Display the version.
.El
.Pp
Byte quantities use unsigned decimal with optional uppercase K, M, G, T or P
binary suffixes.
Signs, whitespace, fractions and overflow fail.
Defaults are 64 GiB scratch, 10,000,000 entries, depth 256, 64 KiB image paths
including separators and NUL, and the depth limit plus 128 owned
descriptors, 384 unless depth is given.
A source tree holds one descriptor per directory level while it is scanned
or copied, and an extraction one per level it creates, so the default fits
a tree as deep as the depth limit, and the 128 cover the rest for about a
hundred operands.
The fds limit also caps the number of create operands: the worker holds one
open root per operand, two for a manifest with a content directory,
besides its scratch directory, the staging directory of an image written
to a file, the raw file of a container, compressed image or stream, and
two scanning descriptors.
The host's own descriptor limit, as
.Ic ulimit -n
shows it, still applies, and
.Nm
never raises it; a tree at the default depth limit needs about 272, more
than the 256 a macOS shell starts with.
Logical extraction output defaults to the selected volume slice length,
including holes.
Work and time have no hidden short default limit.
.Sh READING
Modes
.Cm x , t , W
and
.Cm i
also accept:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl Fl partition Ns = Ns Ar N | Ns Cm name= Ns Ar text | Ns Cm type= Ns Ar name
The partition holding the volume: slot
.Ar N ,
counted from 1; the one GPT partition whose name is
.Ar text ,
compared exactly; or the one partition of a type, given as a type name
(basic, esp, fat12, fat16, fat32, exfat, linux, apfs, hfs, qnx6, qnx4), a GPT
type GUID or an MBR type byte such as 0x0c.
On GPT, basic, fat12, fat16, fat32 and exfat all name the basic-data type, so
each selects any basic-data partition.
Without it the volume is the one partition holding a recognized filesystem;
a disk without a partition table is itself the volume.
.It Fl Fl offset Ns = Ns Ar bytes
Volume offset within the disk; default zero.
.It Fl Fl length Ns = Ns Ar bytes
Positive volume length; default the rest of the disk.
.El
.Pp
.Fl Fl offset
and
.Fl Fl length
select a volume slice by byte range and conflict with
.Fl Fl partition
and with a partition scheme named in
.Fl F .
A range addresses the disk inside any container and never reads the
partition table, so a damaged table does not block it.
Both GPT copies must be intact and agree, MBR entries must lie within the disk
without overlapping, and a table that fails a check fails the command rather
than falling back to the disk as a bare volume.
A disk whose first sectors are both a valid partition table and a filesystem
is refused as ambiguous; force the scheme slot to choose:
.Ql gpt
names the table and
.Ql fat12/-
forces it empty.
QNX6 locates its second superblock in the last whole 4096-byte unit of the
slice, so
.Fl Fl length
must be the exact partition length.
QNX4 derives its block count from the slice length, so
.Fl Fl length
must be its exact partition length too.
QNX6 and QNX4 names compare byte for byte, and hard links list as separate
entries.
QNX4 hides its own
.Pa .bitmap ,
.Pa .inodes ,
.Pa .longfilenames
and
.Pa lost+found .
.Cm W
reports a QNX4 volume whose label is invalid or whose
.Pa lost+found
holds an entry as unsupported, since neither has an observed form to check.
A QNX4 volume whose
.Pa lost+found
.Pa ..
entry names the root record does not open, for the same reason.
An ImageFS is read from the start of the slice, in either byte order; the
startup code and compression of a boot or compressed image are not read.
A parent without a record lists as a directory with mode 0755, owner 0 and
time 0, and of records with the same path only the last lists.
When that last record is a file, symbolic link or FIFO, no record below
its path lists, and both kinds count as shadowed; QNX hides the records
below a symbolic link at boot the same way.
A path with
.Pa \&. ,
.Pa ..
or empty components is unsupported.
.Cm W
checks an ImageFS's additive checksum over the whole image, requires the
volume to end exactly at the image's size, and requires each boot and
script inode reference to name exactly one file, delivered or omitted for
its name; a reference naming only a shadowed record names nothing.
Its result adds, when nonzero, the number of shadowed records, of boot
and script references naming an entry omitted for its name, and of boot
and script references whose inode word, the inode number with its flag
bits, a shadowed record also carries,
which a startup finding them by scanning the table rather than by path
could load instead.
An ImageFS whose mountpoint is outside the name rules is reported as
unsupported, since it has no observed form to check.
.Cm c
writes a nonbootable ImageFS in one layout: the root and an explicit record
for every directory, files, symbolic links and FIFOs with owner, mode and
modification time, each file's data on a 4-byte boundary, and the trailer
checksum.
fsimg does not make an ImageFS bootable: a bootable body, with procnto
linked at its image address and a compiled boot script, must come from
QNX's
.Ql mkifs
or be unwrapped from an existing boot image, and the library's
.Fn fsimg_image_wrap
can then package it in an ifsboot container, its ImageFS bytes unmodified.
Its size follows from its contents, so
.Fl s
and
.Fl Fl free-space
are refused.
A verified ImageFS ends exactly at its image size, so a partition scheme or
a container that pads the volume (DMG, VHD, simg) below
.Ql ifs
is refused before anything is written; gzip compression is not.
QNX 6.5.0
.Ql dumpifs
lists and extracts fsimg's images as it does the
.Ql mkifs
images of the same trees.
.Pp
A gzip-compressed image, a file or standard input, is detected from its
first bytes and decoded, member after member as
.Xr gzip 1
writes them, into the spool, an unlinked file in the scratch tree, which
is then read as the image; standard input that is not compressed is copied
there unchanged, since a container's trailer cannot be reached in a pipe.
Each member's CRC32 and length are checked, and any byte after the last
member, a reserved header flag, a damaged header CRC16 or a file name or
comment longer than 64 KiB fails the command.
The spool's logical size is reserved against
.Fl l Ar spool
(default 64 GiB) before each step of the decode, so a decompression bomb
stops at the step that would cross the limit; runs of zeros are left as
holes.
Decoding counts as work, the compressed bytes read and the bytes decoded,
for a file and standard input alike, so
.Fl l Ar work
bounds it.
When the first bytes pass for both gzip and an uncompressed image, the
input is ambiguous:
.Fl z
reads it as compressed, and
.Fl F ,
or a range or disk selection, which takes the bytes as they are, as not
compressed.
.Fl z
on an input that is not gzip fails.
Standard input is relayed to the worker unread by a separate process and
must not be a terminal.
.Pp
A DMG, a fixed VHD or an Android sparse image is read as the disk it
holds, detected from the file's first bytes and its last 512 bytes, or
forced by naming it in the stack's container slot.
DMG chunks may be stored, zero, free or zlib-compressed; other DMG
compressions (ADC, bzip2, LZFSE, LZMA), multi-segment DMGs and dynamic or
differencing VHDs are refused as unsupported.
A QNX boot image is read as the ImageFS it carries, detected from its
startup header at offset 0 or behind the 1088-byte x86 BIOS loader, or
forced as ifsboot; its body may be raw or LZO1X or NRV2B (UCL) chunks.
Its startup code is never interpreted; writing one is described under
.Cm c .
A container's tables are checked completely when the image opens, and a
damaged one fails the command rather than being read as a raw disk.
Decompressed chunks are cached four at a time, each at most 16 MiB, within
the memory limit; decoding counts as work.
.Pp
A Harman-Becker hbcifs container holds an ImageFS behind a 64-byte header
whose identity is
.Ql hbcifs
and two NUL bytes, as one LZO1X stream that runs to the end of the file.
Its payload length must be the file length minus 64, and the header fields
at 0x10 and 0x18 and the reserved bytes must hold the one producer's
values; the stream is decoded into the spool, reserved against
.Fl l Ar spool
in 64 KiB steps, and must produce exactly the decoded length the header
states.
An hbcifs image that is gzip-compressed or read from standard input would
need a second spool and is refused.
.Pp
.Cm i
reports the stack in
.Fl F
syntax and its compression, gzip or none; with a container, its disk size,
chunk count, chunk encodings and identifier, then its header fields, one
per line in hexadecimal, such as a boot image's startup-header fields and
chunk statistics; with a partition scheme, the disk and each used
partition's slot, type, size, offset, filesystem and name, marking the
selected one; then header validation, geometry, label, volume UUID when the
format has one, the mountpoint and named format details when the format
records them, time basis and known capabilities.
An ImageFS's details are its header's mount flags, boot and script inode
references and chain address, its record counts by inode flag nibble, and
the numbers of implicit directories, shadowed records, omitted subtrees,
references to omitted entries and references a shadowed record also
carries.
When no single partition holds a filesystem,
.Cm i
still reports the table and states that no volume is selected; every other
mode fails.
Unknown counts remain unknown.
.Pp
.Cm t
lists the selected members and their whole subtrees;
.Fl Fl no-recursion
lists one level, the immediate children of a selected directory or the
selected file itself.
.Pp
.Cm W
checks the container, the partition table and the complete supported
structure of the volume, including a DMG's data fork and master checksums
and a boot image's startup and stored checksums and RAM size;
.Fl d
also reads initialized payload bytes and decodes every container chunk to
check the DMG block-table and sparse-image checksums.
A container field no known rule checks, hbcifs's 0x14 and 0x1c, is named as
unverified beside the container and makes the verification incomplete;
every other check still passes or fails the command.
It performs no repair.
.Sh CREATION
Operands are host paths, as for
.Xr tar 1 .
Each is opened relative to the
.Fl C
directory in force, the working directory until a
.Fl C
is given; an absolute operand is opened as given.
Among the operands,
.Fl C Ar dir
or
.Fl C Ns Ar dir
applies to the operands after it.
Each
.Fl C
directory is resolved against the working directory, following symbolic
links as
.Xr tar 1
does, must exist and must apply to at least one operand.
One whose path reaches an existing object of another kind, judged on the
object a symbolic link leads to, fails as unsupported; a dangling or
looping link is an open failure.
An operand is a directory or a regular file; it is opened without following
a final symbolic link, so a symbolic link operand fails.
An operand beginning with @ is a manifest, as
.Sx Manifests
describes.
.Pp
An operand is stored under its own path with empty and
.Pa \&.
components removed, so
.Ql fsimg cf x.img -F fat32 rootfs
produces
.Pa /rootfs/...
and
.Ql -C rootfs \&.
places the contents of rootfs at the image root.
A
.Pa ..
component is a syntax error.
An operand
.Pa a/b/c
is stored as
.Pa /a/b/c
beneath parent directories
.Pa /a
and
.Pa /a/b
that carry no metadata, so the format's defaults for absent fields apply.
Directories supplied by several operands become one directory with the union
of their children; its metadata comes from the first operand that scanned it,
and a parent created for a longer operand never outranks a scanned directory.
Any other repeated image path fails planning and names the path, so naming
the same tree twice fails on its first file.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl e Ar epoch
Signed decimal Unix seconds for reproducible timestamps; requires
.Fl R .
Default is 315532800 (1980-01-01 UTC).
No implicit timezone or SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH policy applies.
.It Fl Fl free-space Ns = Ns Ar bytes
Minimum free space after metadata and allocation rounding.
.It Fl L Ar label
Volume label.
The default FSIMG applies only to a format with a volume label; a format
without one is created without a label and rejects
.Fl L .
.It Fl n
Collect, validate and report a plan without creating an output image or reading
file payloads;
.Fl f
is then optional.
Planning refuses an image larger than the output limit, as creation does;
the raw copy an unencoded image on standard output is written from is
charged only when it is written.
Scratch use is allowed.
.It Fl o Ar sector=bytes
Sector size within the selected profile.
.It Fl o Ar cluster=bytes
Allocation-unit size within the selected profile; for QNX6 the block size,
512, 1024 (the default), 2048 or 4096; for ImageFS, which addresses bytes,
1.
.It Fl o Ar endian=little|big
QNX6 and ImageFS on-disk byte order; default little.
.It Fl o Ar uuid=uuid
QNX6 volume UUID as RFC 4122 text, stored in textual byte order.
Without it,
.Fl R
selects all zero and otherwise a random version 4 UUID is generated.
.It Fl o Ar inodes=count
QNX6 inode capacity, rounded up to a whole inode-table block; default the
native formatter's ratio of one inode per 8 to 128 blocks, raised to the
number of entries.
For QNX4, the
.Pa .inodes
slot count, rounded up to a whole block of 8 slots; default and minimum
128, raised to the number of names longer than 16 bytes plus one.
.It Fl o Ar reserve=percent
QNX6 reserved-block percentage recorded in the header and kept free; default
3.
.It Fl o Ar mountpoint=path
The stored mount path of a format that has one, within the format's length
limit;
.Ar mountpoint=
alone stores an empty one.
ImageFS stores one of at most 1024 bytes of UTF-8 text without control
bytes; default
.Pa / .
.It Fl p
Import each source object's owner, group and permission bits as optional
metadata.
The worker that reads the source runs with a fixed environment and, on
Linux, queries descriptors with direct system calls, so an
.Ev LD_PRELOAD
interposer such as
.Xr fakeroot 1
does not reach it and is not a way to change them; an mtree manifest
.Pq Ar @file
is the way to give an image root or any other ownership.
.It Fl R
Reproducible bytes with stable ordering, identifiers and padding.
.It Fl s Ar bytes
Positive exact logical volume size; omission chooses automatic sizing.
.It Fl W
Verify the staged image in a second worker under the image-reading profile
before publication: the container first, then the partition table, then
the volume;
.Fl d
adds payload reads.
A failed verification discards the staged image, names the layer that
failed and publishes nothing.
.El
.Ss Manifests
An operand
.Ar @file
names an
.Xr mtree 5
manifest, as an @ operand names an archive for
.Xr tar 1 :
.Ql fsimg cf disk.img -F qnx6 -C rootfs @METALOG .
The file is opened relative to the
.Fl C
directory in force, or as given when absolute, without following a
final symbolic link, and must be a regular file.
The manifest's entries are rooted at the image root and merge with the
other operands as their directories do.
That
.Fl C
directory is the manifest's content directory:
a file entry reads its bytes from the file beneath it at its
.Cm contents
path or at its own path, walked without following any link and never
leaving the directory.
That file is opened without blocking on a FIFO and must then be a regular
file; a device node an entry names is opened before it is refused.
Nothing beneath it is read unless an entry needs host bytes.
A manifest given before any
.Fl C
has no content directory, not even the working directory: its generated
files need none, and an entry that needs host bytes fails, naming its line
and path.
Any file beneath a content directory may be named by an entry, so a
manifest from an untrusted source should be given a
.Fl C
directory that holds only what it may read, or none: a
.Fl C
applies to every later operand, so such a manifest comes before the first
one.
A host object whose name begins with @ is named
.Pa ./@name ;
an @ word is a manifest after
.Fl Fl
too.
A manifest operand holds one descriptor, and its content directory one
more, against the fds limit.
.Pp
The manifest is read as untrusted input.
Lines may continue with a final backslash; comments, the
.Ql #mtree
signature,
.Ql /set ,
.Ql /unset ,
.Ql ..
and both relative and full entries are read, and names use the
.Ql \eooo
escapes of
.Xr vis 3 .
Every directory must have its own line, and a path may appear only once,
except a directory repeated with the same keywords.
The keywords used are
.Cm type
.Pq dir, file, link or fifo ,
.Cm uid
and
.Cm gid
together,
.Cm mode ,
.Cm time ,
.Cm size ,
.Cm link ,
.Cm contents
and
.Cm sha256digest
.Pq or Cm sha256 .
.Cm nlink ,
.Cm inode ,
.Cm device ,
.Cm resdevice ,
.Cm tags ,
.Cm uname ,
.Cm gname
beside a numeric id and
.Cm flags=none
are checked and ignored; any other keyword fails.
Owner, mode and time come only from the keywords, whatever
.Fl p
says, and
.Fl R
replaces every time with the epoch as for host files.
.Cm fsimg.content Ns = Ns Cm zero ,
.Cm seeded
or
.Cm positional
generates a file's bytes from its
.Cm size ,
.Cm fsimg.seed
and optional
.Cm fsimg.holes
zero ranges instead of reading them; other tools do not read these
keywords.
A host file's
.Cm sha256digest
is checked as its bytes are copied, so
.Fl n
does not check it; a generated file's is checked by planning, by
.Fl n
too.
Checking a generated file's digest takes time in proportion to the file's
logical size, which the planned volume and
.Fl l Ar output
bound, since no writer stores a file sparsely; the check runs only once
the image has been planned.
Writing may skip a run of zeros and hashing does not, so
.Fl l Ar work
and
.Fl l Ar seconds
bound the time;
.Fl n
hashes the same bytes without writing.
A refused line is named by number.
.Pa specs/design-mtree.md
in the source distribution defines the subset.
.Ss Partition schemes
A stack naming gpt or mbr, such as
.Ql fat32/gpt ,
writes a disk holding the volume as its one partition.
The partition starts at 1 MiB, a whole number of sectors long, with the
GPT's backup table after it; every byte outside the tables and the volume
is zero.
The disk length is derived from the volume, or fixed with
.Ar gpt:disk
or
.Ar mbr:disk ;
a fixed disk without
.Fl s
gives the volume all the space the partition can hold.
The plan
.Pq Fl n
reports the disk length, the volume's offset and length, and every
identifier.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o Ar gpt:type=name|guid
The partition type: a name from the type list under
.Sx READING
or RFC 4122 text.
Default basic data for FAT and exFAT and the QNX6 Power-Safe type for QNX6;
QNX4 has no GPT type and must name one.
.It Fl o Ar gpt:name=text
The partition name, at most 36 UTF-16 units; default the type's
conventional name, such as
.Ql Microsoft basic data .
.It Fl o Ar gpt:disk-guid=guid , Ar gpt:guid=guid
The disk GUID and the partition's unique GUID as RFC 4122 text, not nil.
.It Fl o Ar gpt:sector=512|4096
The sector size of the tables; default 512.
.It Fl o Ar gpt:disk=bytes , Ar mbr:disk=bytes
The exact disk length, a multiple of the sector size; an MBR disk holds at
most 2^32 sectors (2 TiB).
.It Fl o Ar mbr:type=name|0xNN
The partition type byte; default 0x01 for FAT12, 0x06 for FAT16, 0x0c for
FAT32, 0x07 for exFAT, 0xb2 for QNX6 and 0x4d for QNX4.
Extended types (0x05, 0x0f and 0x85) and the GPT protective type 0xee are
refused.
.It Fl o Ar mbr:active=yes|no
Mark the partition active; default no.
.It Fl o Ar mbr:id=0xNNNNNNNN
The 32-bit disk identifier.
.El
.Pp
Without
.Fl R
each identifier not given is random: version 4 GUIDs and a random MBR
identifier.
With
.Fl R
each is derived from the epoch: the epoch's eight little-endian bytes, a
role byte (1 for the disk, 2 for a partition), the partition's slot number
and six zero bytes, with the version 4 and RFC 4122 variant bits set; the
MBR identifier is the epoch's low 32 bits.
Two reproducible disks built at the same epoch therefore share their
identifiers; set them explicitly to attach such disks side by side.
.Ss Containers, compression and streams
A stack naming dmg, vhd or simg, such as
.Ql fat32/gpt/dmg ,
writes the disk inside that container.
The disk is emitted to a private raw file first, then encoded in one
sequential pass that inspects it in chunks: an all-zero chunk becomes a DMG
zero run, an unwritten range of a VHD or a sparse image DONT_CARE chunk,
and a run of them one entry.
A chunk of the raw file that was never written is not read, since fsimg
records the ranges it writes into its own files; neither is a chunk an
input container declares zero, nor a hole of a spooled input.
The raw file and the image are both charged to the output limit; an
explicit
.Fl l Ar output
below their sum fails at planning, naming both.
The disk is rounded up to the container's granularity, which the plan
reports as padding, with the GPT backup header at its last sector.
A DMG is a flattened UDIF image with one block table for the whole disk,
CRC32 table, data fork and master checksums and image variant 2;
.Xr hdiutil 1
verifies and attaches it.
A VHD is the disk and a fixed-disk footer.
A sparse image is its header, its chunks and an optional CRC32 chunk.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o Ar dmg:compress=zlib|none
Store data chunks zlib-compressed (UDZO) or raw (UDRO); default zlib.
A chunk that does not shrink is stored raw.
.It Fl o Ar dmg:level=1..9
The zlib level; default 6; refused with
.Ar compress=none .
.It Fl o Ar dmg:chunk=bytes
The chunk size, a multiple of 512 up to 16 MiB; default 1 MiB.
.It Fl o Ar dmg:uuid=guid , Ar vhd:uuid=guid
The DMG segment ID and the VHD unique ID, as RFC 4122 text, not nil.
.It Fl o Ar vhd:align=bytes
Round the disk up to this power of two from 512 bytes to 1 GiB; default
512.
.It Fl o Ar simg:block=bytes
The block size, a power of two from 1 KiB to 1 MiB; default 4096.
.It Fl o Ar simg:crc=yes|no
End the image with a CRC32 chunk of the expanded disk; default no.
.El
.Pp
An ifsboot container packages the ImageFS of
.Ql ifs/ifsboot
as a QNX boot image: an optional boot prefix, a startup header fsimg
writes, the startup code and the body, raw or in LZO1X chunks of 64 KiB.
The body is the nonbootable ImageFS
.Cm c
writes, whatever the machine.
The startup code and the prefix are copied from files and never parsed;
each is padded with zeros to a multiple of 4 bytes, and fsimg derives every
size and both checksums.
A machine is written only under the modes a native boot of a written
image has passed: this version writes machine 0, the nonbootable output of
QNX's mkifs, under either mode, and machine 3 (x86) under
.Cm none
only, since QNX 6.5.0's x86 startup does not decode an LZO1X body; it
refuses machine 3 under
.Cm lzo
and machine 42 (SH-4) under either mode, naming the machine and the mode.
The settings without a default are required, since a guessed address boots
nothing; each value is stored verbatim and checked only against its table,
and a big-endian ImageFS is refused.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o Ar ifsboot:startup=path
The linked startup code that follows the header; absent, the startup
section is the header and its checksum word alone.
.It Fl o Ar ifsboot:prefix=path
The boot prefix before the header, such as the x86 BIOS loader: padded, it
must be 1088 bytes, and it must not begin with the startup signature;
empty or absent, there is none.
.It Fl o Ar ifsboot:machine=number
The machine: 0, 3 or 42.
.It Fl o Ar ifsboot:virtual=yes|no
The virtual-addressing bit.
.It Fl o Ar ifsboot:startup_vaddr=0xN , Ar ifsboot:paddr_bias=0xN , \
Ar ifsboot:image_paddr=0xN , Ar ifsboot:ram_paddr=0xN , \
Ar ifsboot:imagefs_paddr=0xN
The five target addresses.
.It Fl o Ar ifsboot:compress=lzo|none
The body's compression; default lzo.
.El
.Pp
A file setting's path is resolved against the working directory, never
against
.Fl C ;
fsimg opens it without following a symbolic link before the worker
starts, requires a regular file, and fails the image if the file changes
before the output is complete.
Every read mode refuses the keys.
.Fl i
reports a boot image's header fields under these names, so those values,
with the startup code and prefix the library's
.Fn fsimg_image_unwrap
writes back as sections, rebuild it.
.Pp
Container identifiers follow the scheme's rule: random without
.Fl R ,
derived from the epoch with role byte 3 for the VHD and 4 for the DMG under
it.
The VHD timestamp is the creation time, or the epoch under
.Fl R .
Reproducible zlib output, DMG chunks and gzip alike, depends on the zlib
version, which the plan and
.Fl -formats
name.
.Pp
.Fl f Ar -
writes the image to standard output instead of a file, through a raw file
in the scratch tree, which is charged to
.Fl l Ar output
with the image even without a container;
it is refused when standard output is a terminal, and
.Fl W ,
.Fl a ,
.Fl -json
and
.Fl T Ar -
are syntax errors with it.
A stream has no publication: a failure after any byte was written reports
the output incomplete, and the receiver must discard what it got.
.Pp
.Fl z ,
or
.Fl a
with an image name ending in
.Pa .gz ,
compresses whatever the stack writes, bare volume, disk or container, into
one gzip member, through the same raw file; the raw file and the
compressed image are both charged to
.Fl l Ar output .
The member has no name, comment or header CRC; its time is the creation
time, or the epoch under
.Fl R ,
and zero when the time is not between 1970 and 2106; its extra flags name
level 1 or 9, and its OS byte is 3 (Unix).
With
.Fl a ,
a
.Pa .dmg ,
.Pa .vhd
or
.Pa .simg
suffix, under any compression suffix, must name the stack's container,
and with
.Fl z
the name must end in
.Pa .gz ;
.Pa .zst
and
.Pa .xz
select compressions this version cannot write.
.Cm cW
reads a compressed image back through the spool, so it needs scratch and
.Fl l Ar spool
room for the uncompressed image; a compressed disk without a container
that the limit cannot hold is refused once the creation plans it.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o Ar gzip:level=1..9
The DEFLATE level; default 6.
Only with
.Fl z
or a
.Pa .gz
name under
.Fl a .
.El
.Pp
Import accepts regular files, directories and symbolic links, hierarchy,
content and representable timestamps.
A symbolic link is recorded with its target, which is never followed; a target
longer than 1024 bytes fails the import, and planning fails for a target
longer than the selected format stores, 1023 bytes for QNX4 and QNX6.
The Linux sandbox denies the worker the path-based queries that read a link,
since they are not confined beneath the source, so a confined Linux import of
a tree containing one fails as unsupported, naming the link.
A manifest that describes each link with
.Ql type=link
imports it confined;
.Fl S Cm off
runs the whole creation worker without OS confinement, for trusted sources
and manifests only.
Import rejects special files, including FIFOs.
It passes over its own scratch and staging directories when they lie inside
the tree it imports, so an image can be created inside its source directory;
an operand that names one of them is an error.
Like
.Xr tar 1 ,
it crosses mount points: a filesystem mounted beneath an operand is imported
as part of it.
Hardlinked host files are copied as independent files.
Keep source trees stable; hostile mutation requires immutable storage or a
snapshot.
A changed or replaced source file or link fails creation.
.Pp
A format that cannot store an entry kind fails planning, so FAT and exFAT
reject trees containing symbolic links.
A metadata field a format does not store, such as an owner or mode imported
with
.Fl p
on FAT and exFAT, is an optional loss counted in the usual notice and does not
change the image bytes.
The source-root sandbox cannot universally prevent opening device nodes already
within its authority, a device file system mounted beneath an operand
included: a node is opened before its kind is refused.
Use isolated source storage mounted without devices, with no mount beneath it
that allows them, when that exclusion is required.
.Pp
The image file is created only after planning.
Payloads are copied once to private staging on the destination filesystem.
Publication never replaces an existing target.
It hard-links the staged image to its name or, on a file system without hard
links such as FAT, renames it without replacement where the host offers that;
where it does not, as for exFAT on macOS, the staged image is retained.
Failures distinguish retained staging, published output and uncertain
durability.
Do not retry a published or uncertain outcome blindly.
.Sh EXTRACTION
.Fl C Ar dir
names an existing directory, by default the working directory.
Without
.Fl E
a symbolic link to a directory is followed, and an existing object of
another kind, judged on the object the link leads to, fails as
unsupported; a dangling or looping link is an open failure.
With
.Fl E
a symbolic link fails as unsupported too.
.Cm x
creates exactly one fresh directory inside it, named after the image, with
mode 0700, and extracts beneath that:
.Ql fsimg xf boot.img
extracts into
.Pa ./boot/ .
An existing name of any kind, including an empty directory or a symbolic
link, fails; there is no merge.
The name is the basename of the
.Fl f
path with suffixes removed from the outside in, each at most once and
ASCII case-insensitively: one compression suffix
.Pq Pa .gz ,
then one container suffix
.Pq Pa .dmg , .vhd , .simg ,
then one image suffix
.Pq Pa .img , .raw , .bin , .dd .
.Pa disk.img.gz
yields
.Pa disk
and
.Pa rootfs.vhd.img
yields
.Pa rootfs.vhd .
When nothing was removed, or the rest is empty,
.Pa \&. ,
.Pa ..
or not one valid host name, no name is invented and
.Fl E
is required.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl E
Extract directly into the
.Fl C
directory, which must be empty and private.
.It Fl k
Skip entries of unsupported kinds and omit entries with names outside the
profile instead of failing; the numbers skipped and omitted are reported in
warnings and in the JSON summary.
.It Fl Fl mtree Ns = Ns Ar file
Also write an
.Xr mtree 5
manifest of the extraction to
.Ar file ,
a new name relative to the working directory, as
.Fl f
names an image; see
.Sx Manifests of an extraction .
.It Fl O
Write one member's logical bytes to stdout instead of extracting.
.El
.Pp
Both destinations must be private, judged on the opened directory alone: a
directory owned by the effective user, not writable by group or others, and
carrying no non-trivial access control list of any type the platform reports.
The rule keeps other writers out: one could otherwise swap the directory
before
.Nm
opens it, plant entries the extraction collides with, or replace a staged
image or
.Fl -mtree
file between its validation and its publication.
Read and search permission for others is allowed; who can read what
.Nm
writes follows the filesystem's permissions: the modes
.Nm
asks for on entries it creates, or the mount's permissions on a filesystem
that ignores them, such as vfat.
With
.Fl E
the directory is opened without following a symbolic link and must also be
empty; a symbolic link or any other existing object that is not a directory
fails as unsupported.
A fresh directory that inherits a non-trivial ACL from its parent is refused
and the error names the parent; fsimg removes it again, and reports it as
possibly retained when the removal is denied.
The private directories that stage a created image or an
.Fl -mtree
file, and the scratch directory, are checked the same way, so an image or
.Fl -mtree
file cannot be written into a directory that gives new directories a
non-trivial ACL, or onto a filesystem without POSIX permissions, such as FAT
mounted on macOS, which reports write permission for everyone; write the
image or extract with
.Fl C
elsewhere and copy the result, and while scratch is in such a directory
every command fails until
.Fl T
names another.
.Nm
never edits an ACL.
On macOS, an ACL entry that gives another user the right to change a
directory's permissions or ACL leaves a race the check does not close:
that user can start such a change, remove the entry before the check, and
have the change land after it, leaving the directory writable by them.
So on macOS, give the extraction
.Fl C ,
.Fl f ,
.Fl -mtree
or
.Fl T
no parent whose inheritable ACL grants that right, and give
.Fl E
no directory that carries, or was created with, such an entry.
On Linux a
.Ql system.nfs4_acl
attribute counts as non-trivial whatever it holds, so neither a destination
nor an image,
.Fl -mtree
file or scratch directory can be placed on an NFS server with ACL support,
and the server-side ACLs of a CIFS mount are not visible to the check at all.
.Pp
The check trusts the owner and permissions the filesystem reports, so a
filesystem that does not enforce them defeats it, and the modes
.Nm
gives what it creates protect nothing there.
For example, a volume that ignores ownership, such as an external APFS or
HFS+ volume on macOS, where that is the default and
.Xr mount 8
lists it as
.Ql noowners ,
shows every directory as the caller's while every local user can write it:
the destination, staging and scratch directories pass, yet other users can
read or replace what
.Nm
writes there.
Separately, a filesystem that ignores the modes
.Nm
asks for on new files, such as Linux vfat mounted with a permissive fmask,
can hold a directory that passes while the files
.Nm
creates in it are writable by others.
Keep output, staging and scratch off such filesystems when other users are
not trusted.
.Pp
Selected paths and necessary ancestors are preserved below the image root.
Output uses private directories and regular files, no links, no merge,
no privileged metadata and no host timestamp restoration.
Every entry is created exclusively, so an object another process adds
concurrently is a collision failure.
An image entry of another kind, such as a symbolic link or FIFO, fails the
extraction and is named in the error, unless
.Fl k
skips it.
So does an entry a QNX4, QNX6 or ImageFS reader omits because its name,
though QNX accepts it, lies outside the profile: strict UTF-8 without
control bytes or DEL, unless
.Fl k
omits it.
.Pp
.Fl O
takes exactly one member, a regular file or a symbolic link, whose bytes are
its target, and writes its raw logical bytes including zero tails.
It rejects
.Fl Fl json , Fl Fl mtree , C , E
and
.Fl k .
.Pp
Logical holes count against the output quota.
Failure can leave a private incomplete destination, identified by diagnostics.
There is no directory-wide atomic publication or resume promise.
If only final reporting fails, the destination may already contain complete
content; the command still reports failure and conservatively marks its output
incomplete.
.Ss Manifests of an extraction
With
.Fl Fl mtree ,
the manifest records what the extracted tree cannot hold, so that the tree
and the manifest re-create the image:
.Ql fsimg xkf rootfs.img --mtree=rootfs.mtree
followed by
.Ql fsimg cf copy.img -F qnx6 -C rootfs @../rootfs.mtree .
It has one line per extracted entry, in the image's order, after the
.Ql #mtree v2.0
signature and the root's
.Ql \&. type=dir
line: the path in the full form
.Pa ./dir/name ,
then
.Cm type ,
.Cm mode ,
.Cm uid
and
.Cm gid ,
and
.Cm time
as the image stores them,
.Cm size
and
.Cm sha256digest
for a file, and
.Cm link
for a symbolic link.
A field the image does not store is left out.
The digest is computed from the bytes written as they are copied.
An entry
.Fl k
skips, such as a symbolic link or FIFO, is still listed, without a digest;
the manifest is its only record.
An entry
.Fl k
omits for a name outside the profile is never visited and has no line, so
the re-created image lacks it.
A FAT or exFAT time without a zone is written as UTC.
Every byte of a path or link target outside printable ASCII, and every
.Ql \e
and
.Ql # ,
is written as a
.Ql \eooo
escape of
.Xr vis 3 ,
so every line is plain ASCII and reads back exactly.
An escape takes four bytes.
No limit of its own bounds the manifest, nor a
.Cm t
listing:
.Fl l Ar entries
bounds their lines, and
.Fl l Ar path
and the 1024-byte link-target limit cap each line, so a manifest or
listing of an image of many entries beneath long non-ASCII names can be
many times larger than the image.
Only standard keywords appear: bsdtar and the
.Xr mtree 8
of FreeBSD and NetBSD read it; the macOS
.Xr mtree 8
reads only relative entries and does not.
.Pp
The manifest is staged in a private directory beside its name and
published with mode 0600, without replacement, only after the extraction
completes; a failed extraction leaves no manifest, and an existing name
fails before anything is extracted.
A publication that is unsupported or uncertain retains the staged manifest,
and a cleanup that fails retains its private directory; either is reported
as the retained manifest.
A failure reported after publication, such as a durability failure, leaves
the manifest in place and reports the output published.
It holds one descriptor against the fds limit.
.Sh OUTPUT
Human names and controls are escaped.
A backslash shows as
.Ql \e\e
in listings and errors alike.
In an error, a name outside the profile shows each byte outside 0x20 to 0x7e
as
.Ql \exNN ;
an error path too long to report keeps its end and is labeled truncated.
A JSON entry path holds the name itself, while a JSON error path holds the
same rendering, with
.Ql \e\e
for a backslash.
Listings, extraction with
.Fl k
and verification never show an omitted entry; a warning,
.Dq N entries with names outside the profile were omitted ,
and the JSON summary's omitted_entries count each omitted subtree once.
Diagnostics and bounded progress use stderr; payloads and records use stdout.
Successful creation and extraction are silent by default.
Listings contain one quoted pathname per line, without a completion trailer;
a symbolic link is marked with
.Ql @
and a FIFO with
.Ql |
after the quoted name.
The long listing of
.Cm t Fl v
prefixes each name with three columns: the kind
.Pq Ql - , d , l No or Ql p
followed by nine permission characters, or nine
.Ql \&?
when the format stores no mode; the logical length of a file or link, or
.Ql - ;
and the modification time as
.Ql YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS
in UTC, the recorded calendar fields for an unknown-offset time, or
.Ql -
when absent.
It consults no locale or host timezone.
Information and dry-run creation plans use aligned labels and binary byte
units.
Unknown free space is labeled without scanning the filesystem.
.Fl Fl formats
displays a compact read/write/verify table; use
.Fl v
for detailed restrictions.
Creation plans appear on stdout with
.Fl n
or on stderr with
.Fl vv .
Verification reports the checked scope only after successful worker exit and
finalization.
.Pp
On success, optional metadata loss produces one notice counting affected fields,
not files.
Verbose examples are sampled and truncated path excerpts are labeled.
Failed commands do not append metadata-loss notices to their error reports.
Sandbox warnings remain visible before processing.
Errors explain the failure and any relevant OS error without numeric errno
fields.
Actual cleanup failures, retained paths, incomplete output and uncertain
publication or durability are always reported.
.Pp
JSON entries stream individually and are followed by a successful summary only
after worker completion and required finalization.
An error record or missing successful summary means incomplete output.
The summary's command field names the mode:
.Ql c ,
.Ql cW ,
.Ql x ,
.Ql t ,
.Ql W ,
.Ql i
or
.Ql formats .
A stack record precedes the information record: it carries the stack, the
compression (gzip or null), the container with its chunk count, chunk
encodings, identifier, null without one, and container_details, an object
of each named header field's decimal string value, empty for a container
without header fields, all null without a container, the scheme, the disk
size and identifier
and the selected partition, and states whether a volume was selected.
Partition records, one per used slot, follow it for
.Cm i .
A creation's plan record follows the planned disk's stack record and its
one partition record when the stack names a scheme.
Information records carry the stack and the compression.
.Cm cW
writes a verify record for its staged image.
A verify record's complete member is false, and its unverified member
names the fields, when the container holds fields no known rule checks.
Large integers are decimal strings; bounded geometry, flags, nanoseconds and
offset minutes are JSON integers.
Unknown values are null.
Timestamp records state basis and precision.
Entry records name their kind as file, directory, symlink or fifo and carry
change time, uid, gid and mode, each null when absent.
Information records also give the mountpoint, null when absent, and details,
an object of each named format detail's decimal string value.
Format records name their kind: filesystem, scheme, container or
compression.
A filesystem's record gives entry_kinds, metadata_fields and write_settings
as bit masks, max_label_bytes, zero for a format without a label, and
max_link_target_bytes, zero for a format without symbolic links; a layer's
gives its suffix, its capabilities, the keys of its settings, the chunk
encodings it reads as codecs and, for a layer using zlib, the zlib version as
codec_version.
.Pp
The operation deadline spans processing and successful output.
Failure reporting has a two-second grace and one further two-second stderr-only
attempt if stdout is unusable.
Neither attempt resumes processing or publication.
Scratch cleanup removes at most 4096 flat entries, never follows links or
recurses into worker-created directories, and reports retained objects.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Status 1 means operational failure, including corruption, unsupported required
features, cancellation, partial output or uncertain durability.
Status 2 means invalid command syntax.
A sandbox-unavailable warning under automatic mode alone is not failure.
.Pp
.Nm
recognizes the following and reports each as not supported, with status 1,
after the rest of the command line is checked:
writing zstd or xz
.Pq Fl a No with Cm c ,
minimization
.Pq Fl M No with Cm c ,
and the reserved filesystem names apfs and hfs.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fsimg cf boot.img -F fat32 -L BOOT -s 64M -C esp .
fsimg cRf data.img -F exfat -C files .
fsimg cWf data.img -F exfat -C esp . -C extra EFI
fsimg cRf disk.img -F qnx6 -C rootfs @METALOG
fsimg cn -F exfat -R -m 64M -T /scratch -C files .
fsimg cWf esp.img -F fat32/gpt -o gpt:type=esp -o gpt:name=EFI -C esp .
fsimg cf sd.img -F fat32/mbr -o mbr:disk=4G -o mbr:active=yes -C boot .
fsimg tf sd.img.gz
curl -s https://example.org/sd.img.gz | fsimg xf - -E -C restored
fsimg tf data.img --json /
fsimg tvf data.img
fsimg xOf data.img /documents/readme.txt > readme.txt
fsimg xf data.img -S required -C /tmp /documents
fsimg xEf data.img -C ./empty-dir
fsimg Wdf data.img
fsimg if disk.img --offset=1M --length=1G
fsimg tf disk.img --partition=2 /EFI
fsimg xf disk.img --partition=name=EFI
fsimg tf installer.dmg -F fat32/gpt/dmg
fsimg Wdf system.simg
fsimg Wf disk.img -F fat32/gpt -o gpt:sector=4096
fsimg --formats --json
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr tar 1 ,
.Xr libfsimg 3 ,
.Xr mtree 5
